Package: STATassist
Title: Standardised Statistical Comparison Workflows
Version: 1.0.0
Authors@R: 
    person("Wonseok", "Oh", , "hiows97@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0009-0002-0687-8466"))
Description: Runs every test applicable to a comparison through a single
    function call and returns standardised, feature-wise result tables. One
    sample, two groups and three or more groups each have one function, and each
    reports parametric, rank-based and robust results side by side, together with
    fold changes, effect estimates, confidence intervals and multiplicity
    adjusted p-values, so that a conclusion is never based on a single test.
    Multi-group comparison pairs each omnibus test with the post-hoc procedure
    that shares its assumptions. Crossed factors have a function of their own
    that fits a single two-way, three-way or factorial model, reports the answer
    per model term as well as per feature, and compares levels on the unweighted
    marginal means and inside each combination of the remaining factors.
    Two categorical variables, or repeated binary conditions on the same row,
    have a function of their own that returns a contingency table rather than a
    feature-wise comparison, with the chi-square test beside Fisher's exact test
    or McNemar's test or Cochran's Q. That result names the null hypothesis it
    tested, independence or symmetry or marginal homogeneity, and its expected
    counts and residuals are read under that one, so the mosaic plot of it shades
    departure from the hypothesis the p-value beside it is about.
    Assumption diagnostics are attached to every
    result and never change which tests run. Significance tables and volcano and
    forest visualisations are derived from the same result object, which keeps
    effect sizes and p-values describing the same observations, while boxplot,
    clustered heatmap and back-to-back histogram visualisations read the wide
    input the comparisons take. Supervised models for a continuous or a two-class
    outcome, and principal component, t-SNE and UMAP embeddings, return two further
    contracts built the same way, with one train/test partition function ahead of
    them that keeps a sampling unit on one side of the split. One further pair of
    functions scores any number of those fitted models on the same held-out rows,
    keeping only the rows every model could predict so that the paired comparisons
    of discrimination, reclassification and calibration describe one sample, and
    returns them beside a scatter of the predictions or overlaid ROC curves.
    Simulators return
    two-group, multi-group, crossed-factor and supervised learning data together
    with the effects and coefficients planted in them, so a verdict can be scored
    against an answer that is known. The factorial simulator states its answer per
    model term, main effects and interactions alike, and marks each factor as
    measured between subjects or within them, which is the answer key the
    factorial comparison is scored against. The categorical simulator plants an
    association between two variables, or a transition between repeated binary
    conditions, and states it cell by cell under the same null hypothesis the
    comparison holds the drawn table against.
    The implemented methods include those described by
    Welch
    (1947) <doi:10.1093/biomet/34.1-2.28>, Wilcoxon (1945)
    <doi:10.2307/3001968>, Mann and Whitney (1947)
    <doi:10.1214/aoms/1177730491>, Kruskal and Wallis (1952)
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1952.10483441>, Friedman (1937)
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1937.10503522>, Tukey (1949) <doi:10.2307/3001913>,
    Dunn (1964) <doi:10.1080/00401706.1964.10490181>, Yuen (1974)
    <doi:10.1093/biomet/61.1.165>, Brunner and Munzel (2000)
    <doi:10.1002/(SICI)1521-4036(200001)42:1%3C17::AID-BIMJ17%3E3.0.CO;2-U>,
    Algina, Keselman and Penfield (2005) <doi:10.1037/1082-989X.10.3.317>,
    DeLong, DeLong and Clarke-Pearson (1988) <doi:10.2307/2531595>, Sun and Xu
    (2014) <doi:10.1109/LSP.2014.2337313>, Pencina, D'Agostino, D'Agostino and
    Vasan (2008) <doi:10.1002/sim.2929>, and Pencina, D'Agostino and Steyerberg
    (2011) <doi:10.1002/sim.4085>.
